2291801 damerell-Ran-Orc-Mal-Cha ascended to demigod-hood.  176 [257]

Ever since I begun to play NetHack - going on a decade ago - I had the
ambition of eventually ascending every role; I suspect every player does.
Since then one role has left the game (elves; but I've done one) and two
have been added; and the tournament has finally provided me with an
incentive to ascend this ranger, my seventeenth ascension - and the first
one who has not been a female character called Kirsty. I'm shooting for
all races/genders/alignments, so this ranger was Chaotic and orcish; the
cloak of displacement makes human and orcish rangers stronger than elven
ones, and someone this month is going to have to be an orc.

An altar on DL 2 made for a reasonably straighforward start with Stormy
the first weapon granted. I would get Frosty from sacrificing much later,
but be granted no other weapons; this was a bit of pain against demons and
vampires, so I brought a silver saber along and beat on them with that.
However, Stormy did exceptionally well against other beasties; the healing
helps in the early game, and sucking the levels out of spellcasting
adversaries and the Riders doesn't hurt at all. I butchered all peacefuls
with gay abandon.

I also had found a luckstone early, although did not positively identify
it until after clearing the Mines, which meant the early sacfest was also
maxing out my Luck; and same-race sacrifice let me summon Jubilex and
Yeenoghu and ditch them - initially on DL 1, then in the Mines much later
so I could pass without trouble with the amulet.

I did Sokoban first, and had quite a scare when I met a master lich on the
second level. It turned itself invisible as its first move; I ran like mad,
blessing the early wand of speed monster; but a claw/claw/bite attack
sequence let me realise this was definitely a chameleon not a nightmarish
bones pile, so I returned and fought, beating it easily. On the way up
Sokoban I tamed a cat, Kanuka, with a view to robbing shops, but she died
almost immediately. I got the BoH ending, which didn't hurt.

I then pressed down the Mines, clearing them with no difficulty save for a
marilith on the bottom level, which I simply evaded having detected it
with telepathy; I got the wine cellar, which remains the most useful in my
view. At this point I'd found general stores on levels 3 and 5, a bookshop
on level 9, a full complement of mine town shops, and an armoury on level 6;
and I would shortly find a potion shop on level 13. It didn't take me long
to realise, with an ample supple of ID, that shopkeepers were of little
use to me; I'd already butchered the watch in a Stormbringer incident, and
used my stack of daggers (after the very opening stages I only ever used
arrows to take out disenchanters) and Stormy to kill every shopkeeper save
Izchak, netting me a lot of gold and a vast heap of potions, scrolls, and
other goodies.

I returned to the altar for an ID session. I was crowned from a water
prayer; a nuisance in terms of altar-scumming, but since I had gained no
resistances from corpses at all, it probably didn't hurt. Then down to
level 13 to rob a vault I'd found while falling through a trapdoor
earlier; on level 12 I found a huge leprechaun hall, and the loot from
these (and the potion shopkeeper on 13) made for a naked AC 2 which a
later dose of divine protection would improve a point or two. I didn't get
Ludious, but I didn't need it.

I got the bigroom, but had no trouble - and got a free AoESP out of it. I
did get surrounded, but not by very serious adversaries. I had also got a
set of green dragon scales courtesy of a polytrap beyond the lep hall,
which I converted into a GDSM (but not the usual sort) for clothed AC -22;
and a wraith corpse yielded XL 14 well before the quest.

At this point I was starting to feel pretty tooled up; good AC, good
weapon, and starting with a good Con and rapidly raising it to 18 (19 with
a +1 ring for a while) thanks to gain abilities in the potion shop haul
eliminated the usual ranger HP worries; but I was concerned by the lack of
MR and reflection. Luckily, a bones pile on DL 14 from a monk who'd
expired along with the spotted jelly he was fighting removed most of my
concerns; beyond a (quite small, for DL14, but it's _free_) haul of
potions and scrolls, this netted me the unicorn horn I was worried about,
speed boots, an AoR, and a second magic lamp which I successfully used to
wish for grey DSM. Thanks, chickenman!

I then made my way to the Quest, which turned up soon enough. After
hacking up about a hundred centaurs, I met Scorpius; I was concerned with
his sickness attack, since Chaotics can't use holy water to cure it and I
had only a small supply of PoFH; and I made a fairly critical mistake by
flinging a potion of paralysis at him at point-blank range, which
paralysed me but not him. I would have been lifesaved, but fortunately the
few turns' paralysis weren't long enough, so I quaffed one of my PoFH and
simply laid into him. The layout of the Ranger quest is such that you can
easily approach Scorpius while keeping the stairs in LOS, and so when he
teleported back to them I killed him with daggers. Since there was a
polytrap on the level with a horde of monsters trying it out, I left
immediately.

At Medusa (where the titan was a complete pushover with daggers) I had no
means of crossing water easily; I had the spell of levitation at 88%
failure. I trained it up with haste self, and dug down on the basis that
if Perseus didn't have the requisites I could still cross the water in
tiny hops, but Perseus did supply the levitation boots I wanted. Having
the spell of haste self also let me stay Very Fast while I had levitation
boots on (or the water walking boots I found in Vlad's).

The Castle was easy enough, and the wand 0:3 - this left me with a spare
wish (plus the wrestable charge) even though I had had no wishes from
thrones, only one from lamps, no magic markers to that point (although I
would find one in Gehennom), and wished up a spellbook of magic mapping
and a ring of free action (although I would find two in Gehennom). Netting
two scrolls of charging from the bookshop didn't hurt. I returned to my
stash (on DL 7, so a bit of a hike) one more time, then magic mapped and
cleared Gehennom, Vlad's, and the first two levels of the Wizard's Tower;
I got full of loot once (given the haul of books from the Valley), so I
left it in a sack on level 35 which I then used controlled level teleport
to retrieve to my stash without needing to carry it any distance. I got
fed up with fighting liches, so I genocided them with my emergency
genocide scroll; I would find another one, but only after dispatching all
the mind flayers and disenchanters I would encounter, so I never used. The
decision to genocide liches was triggered by a minor summoning storm at
the bottom of Gehennom; other than that I had no problems, and even got
Orcus's wand off him with one charge (which I used on an MMF, so I had
none for Rodney).

At the stash I did the last round of potion-diluting and alchemy and
suchlike; then set off for the surface with a "care package" (the scrolls
of gold detection, a pile of K-rations, some of the holy water and PoFH
etc) and to shepherd Jubilex and Yeenoghu down to the Mines - and to off
Asmodeus, who had level teleported away earlier. Once that was done I
returned to the stash and level teleported to the Castle with two more
care packages; one for the Castle, one for Vlad's. Having dropped those
off I offed the titan in Rodney's top floor and Rodney himself without
trouble, and did the invocation; getting in was easy, but once again I'd
failed to bring a cPoGL and although I was able to easily evade the
priests both ways (hint; don't try and clean out the graveyard, it wastes
a lot of time during which a lot of monsters are generated in the next
room) I had to slug through the graveyard on the way out by killing
zombies and moving through their spaces.

I had little trouble with the mysterious force or with Rodney... except
for the one occasion where he Double-Troubled and summoned Demogorgon
almost immediately on a no-teleport level. After a brief moment of panic
and a longer moment of checking my equipment, I dug into my bag for a
spare AoLS, some PoFH, and my reserve wish, which went on a rubber
chicken; burned one PoFH curing sickness, but stoned the lot of them.
Irritatingly, further up, I forgot that Rodney's corpse can give
teleportitis as well as telecontrol; fortunately I was close enough to the
surface that wearing my ring of telecontrol for those few levels was not a
severe annoyance. I killed a cockatrice on DL4, too, whose corpse lasted
until the Plane of Earth, getting Rodney out of my hair once and for all.

Earth was a doddle as ever (I had a large surplus of WoDig thanks to the
lack of mysterious force woes); Air was very annoying with eight trips
into elementals and one PoFH burned, but not actually threatening; Fire
was tedious but not dangerous; and Water was over in no time at all.

I did Death first - the right-hand altar - and with slick maneuvering and
my large collection of WoTele charges (doesn't work on the Riders, but it
works on everyone _else_) - managed to avoid ever being attacked by her,
but this was the wrong one.

Famine was next on the left; here I couldn't avoid combat, but with an
ample supply of K-rats and satiating myself before the fight, I had no
trouble; Stormy works wonders on Famine and Pestilence, who actually start
missing you after a few goes; I killed Famine twice on the way in (where
my guardian angel died) along with a few friendly priests (oops) and once
on the way out. More worrying was running into an NPC with the Vorpal
Blade, but I teleported him away and never saw him again.

There was already a very heavy press around the entrance to the middle
altar; with plenty of ants as well as a dragon and a vampire, I blew my
one scroll of taming on the confused taming trick, which worked wonders
(although I did off two pets with Stormbringer before remembering to
unwield it to move); I still had to fight Pestilence once, but had a more
than ample supply of curing, and then just displaced and teleported-away
my way to the altar. I remembered to bring my magic whistle this time,
which was very convenient.

More or less everything in the screenshot which is not an Angel or a
priest is a pet of mine; I spent quite a lot of time blind from lightning
strikes, which is why the map is incomplete.

